+
+# Task — JSON Pointer resolution (RFC 6901)
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document. Implement JSON Pointer as
+specified in RFC 6901. Export a function `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`. Given a
+JSON document and a JSON Pointer string, it returns the value the pointer refers to. It throws an error
+when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer, or when it does not resolve to a value of the document.
+
+This is the first of several features that build on each other; later features will use it.
+
+## Scope
+
+- New source file `src/index.ts`, exporting `resolvePointer`.
+- Signature: `resolvePointer(document: unknown, pointer: string): unknown`.
+- Doc comment on the exported function, stating the behaviour and the RFC it implements (directive:
+  documentation).
+- Tests under `test/` runnable with `npm test` (`node --test`).
+- No runtime dependency is added (README).
+
+## Acceptance criteria (RFC 6901)
+
+1. The empty pointer `""` refers to the whole document.
+2. A non-empty pointer must start with `/`; otherwise it is invalid and `resolvePointer` throws.
+3. Reference tokens are split on `/`. In each token, `~1` is decoded to `/` and `~0` to `~`, in that
+   order. A `~` followed by any other character, or at the end of a token, is invalid and throws.
+4. Object: a token selects the member whose name equals the decoded token. The member must be an own
+   property; inherited properties (for example `constructor`, `__proto__`) do not resolve.
+5. Array: a token selects an element only when it is a valid array index: `0`, or a non-zero digit
+   followed by digits (no leading zeros, no sign). The token `-` names the position past the end and does
+   not resolve. An index outside the array throws.
+6. A token that selects into a primitive value (string, number, boolean, null) does not resolve and throws.
+7. A pointer that does not resolve to a value throws. A value that is present but `null`, `false`, `0`
+   or `""` resolves successfully and is returned as is.
+8. A pointer that is not a string throws.
+9. The document is not modified.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- URI fragment form of JSON Pointer (RFC 6901 section 6).
+- Writing or mutating values at a pointer.
